{#
  A module's sub-page navigation — the pages[] → nav_rail mapping layer,
  written once (design.md §7.6 companion).

  Six places used to copy this exact boilerplate (finance's _nav,
  rental's management _nav, the calendar picker, staffs, rental config…),
  a seventh re-drew the row with raw btn-primary pills, and two modules
  used Bootstrap nav-pills — a third visual style for the same concept.
  One include, one component underneath, so every module's sub-navigation
  looks and behaves the same.

  That component is now partials/nav_rail.html.twig — Bootstrap
  `nav nav-underline` + `flex-nowrap` + `overflow-auto`. This is a
  deliberate, approved PARTIAL REVERSAL of UX-convergence decision #4
  ("nav-pills → chips"), not an oversight to correct: chips remain wrong
  for sub-navigation — a chip reads as a filter you toggle, and the
  wrapping-plus-"+N"-sheet behaviour hid pages behind an overflow chip —
  but so were pills, which is what decision #4 was actually reacting to.
  Underlined tabs are the shape the web already uses for "which view of
  this page am I on". See design.md §7.6.

  A fixed set of pages, declared in code, with short labels is exactly
  the nav rail's case rather than the select bar's — see
  docs/module-development.md for the rule.

  Usage:
  {% include 'partials/page_picker.html.twig' with {
      picker_id: 'finance-page-picker',
      pages: [
          { url: '/finance', label: 'Tableau de bord' },
          { url: '/finance/movements', label: 'Mouvements' },
      ],
      current_path: current_path,     -- the global, OR a synthetic path
                                      -- the caller computes when two
                                      -- views share one route and differ
                                      -- by a query (see groups/list and
                                      -- the news form tabs); selection by
                                      -- equality or by prefix (see
                                      -- match_prefix)
      extra_query: '?account_id=3',   -- optional, appended to every link
      aria_label: 'Pages Finances',   -- optional, names the <nav>
      match_prefix: true,             -- optional: a sub-route keeps its
                                      -- parent page selected
  } only %}

  A page may also carry `icon` (a Bootstrap Icons class) and `align_end`
  (push this page, and every page after it, to the far end of the rail —
  for the one entry that leaves the module the rail navigates). Both are
  passed straight through to the nav rail; see its own docblock.
#}
{# Longest match wins — /finance/movements/12 selects « Mouvements »,
   never also « Tableau de bord » because it happens to sit at /finance. #}
{% set _best = '' %}
{% for page in pages %}
    {% set _matches = current_path == page.url
        or (match_prefix|default(false) and page.url != '/' and current_path starts with page.url ~ '/') %}
    {% if _matches and page.url|length > _best|length %}
        {% set _best = page.url %}
    {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% set _items = [] %}
{% for page in pages %}
    {% set _items = _items|merge([{
        id: page.url,
        label: page.label,
        selected: page.url == _best,
        icon: page.icon|default(null),
        align_end: page.align_end|default(false),
    }]) %}
{% endfor %}
<div class="page-picker mb-3">
    {% include 'partials/nav_rail.html.twig' with {
        picker_id: picker_id,
        items: _items,
        base_url: '',
        extra_query: extra_query|default(''),
        aria_label: aria_label|default('Navigation de la page'),
    } %}
</div>
